  <h1>Marketing plan for an architecture firm</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Most firms already have a marketing plan. It is a list of channels, a percentage of revenue,
    and a quiet hope that one of them works. Almost nobody writes down what a single signed client
    is supposed to cost, which is the one line that would make the rest of the page an argument
    instead of a wish.
  </p>

  <p>
    On 16 September 2026 we pulled the live US search result for "architecture firm marketing
    strategy" and read the sources behind Google's own AI answer. They agree on the shape of a plan:
    find your positioning, fix the website, publish content, network, set a budget. Not one of them
    publishes what any of it costs.
  </p>
  <p>
    We can, because we run the ad accounts. What follows is the same plan with a measured price on
    every line, taken from 21 US architecture and design firm accounts over twelve months of spend.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>
      A marketing plan for an architecture firm is four numbers and a sequence before it is a
      channel list. Decide the cost per booked consultation you can live with, fund the one channel
      that can deliver at that price for ninety days, and measure at the lead and again at the
      booked consultation. Across 21 firm accounts over twelve months the median account paid
      $50.41 per lead and $147.76 per booked consultation, and a median full month of spend was
      $1,452 for 22 leads. Build the plan backward from those two figures.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The budget rule Google hands you is missing from the pages it cites</h2>
  <p>
    That search returns an AI Overview above every other result. Its budget line reads "Allocate
    roughly 5% to 10% of your annual revenue to support consistent marketing and lead tracking," and
    it hangs six citations off that one sentence. We opened four of them and read the pages.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Cited source</th><th>Does it state the 5 to 10 percent rule?</th><th>Real cost data on the page?</th></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="https://www.aia.org/aia-architect/article/5-ways-level-your-firm-marketing" rel="nofollow">AIA, "5 ways to level up your firm marketing"</a> (31 Mar 2025)</td><td>No percentage appears anywhere on the page</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="https://www.businessofarchitecture.com/the-ultimate-guide-to-marketing-for-architects-examples-and-case-studies/" rel="nofollow">Business of Architecture, "The Ultimate Guide to Marketing for Architects"</a></td><td>No</td><td>One story: a single ad that cost over £4,500 (about $5,500) and produced no chargeable work</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="https://www.thirdandarch.com/thoughts/the-power-of-architecture-marketing/" rel="nofollow">Third &amp; Arch, "The Power of Architecture Marketing"</a></td><td>No</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="https://www.vernacularagency.com/house-style/marketing-plan-for-architecture-firm" rel="nofollow">Vernacular Agency, "The Architect's Guide to a Winning Marketing Plan"</a></td><td>Yes: "some businesses spend 3-5% of their revenue on marketing, growing firms often invest 7-10%"</td><td>None. Its one figure is a hypothetical $2,000 a month</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Three of the four contain no percentage at all. The fourth carries a range and cites nothing for
    it. The most confident, most actionable sentence in the answer turns out to be a number the
    machine assembled and then attributed to pages that never make the claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of those publishers did anything wrong. The assembly step did, and it is worth knowing
    about before you build a year around a figure you found in an AI answer. We saw the same
    pattern when we logged every source four engines cite about this profession, in <a href="/ai-search-for-architects">AI search for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Four numbers, then channels</h2>
  <p>
    Percent-of-revenue is a comfortable rule because it never has to be right. Spend 6% and you can
    call the year a success whatever happens, since the rule was about the spending. Our view on
    that argument sits in the pillar under <a href="/architecture-marketing#how-much-should-an-architecture-firm-spend-on-marketing">how much an architecture firm should spend</a>, and the short version is that you judge spend by what comes back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four numbers turn a channel list into a plan. What a lead costs. What share of clicks become
    leads. What share of leads become a booked consultation. What a booked consultation costs all
    in. Here is the distribution across 21 accounts, 15 September 2025 to 11 September 2026, each
    account counted once so a single big spender cannot set the median.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Better quarter (p25)</th><th>Median</th><th>Harder quarter (p75)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost per lead</td><td>$28.25</td><td>$50.41</td><td>$70.58</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Click to lead</td><td>12.52%</td><td>9.10%</td><td>5.55%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lead to booked consultation</td><td>44.7%</td><td>32.5%</td><td>15.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost per booked consultation</td><td>$104.78</td><td>$147.76</td><td>$239.57</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Read the spread rather than the middle. The gap between a good account and a struggling one is
    roughly 2.3x on cost per booked consultation, and almost none of that gap comes from the ad
    platform. Offer, creative and how fast somebody calls the lead back do most of the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full method, sample and caveats are in the <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#the-benchmark-table">architecture marketing benchmark report</a>, and if you want these figures applied to a budget you are actually weighing, the <a href="/architecture-marketing-budget-calculator">budget calculator</a> does the arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p>
    One caution on stacking quartiles. Multiplying a p25 cost per lead by a p25 booking rate
    produces a number no account in the data has ever hit. Each figure above is measured directly
    for the metric it describes.
  </p>

  <h2>What a normal month looks like, so the plan survives one</h2>
  <p>
    A median full month of spend across those accounts was $1,452.56 producing 22 leads. The quieter
    quartile ran $1,141 for 18, the busier one $2,534 for 40. That is the unit the plan is built
    from, and it is a smaller number than most firms brace for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Leads arrive faster than firms expect and then stop for days at a time. Median time to first
    lead was one day, 17 of the 21 accounts had one inside week one, and the slowest took twelve
    days. Meanwhile 747 of 1,839 live account-days produced no leads at all, which is 40.6% of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Write that into the plan explicitly. A quiet Tuesday and a quiet Wednesday are the ordinary
    texture of this. Two silent weeks with spend running is a real signal, and the difference
    between those two states is the thing worth putting a review date against.
  </p>

  <h2>Start in the cheapest quarter you can reach</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve months of data bought one finding that a shorter window cannot produce. Indexing every
    account-month against that same account's own annual cost per lead, so market and offer cancel
    out, the seasons separate cleanly.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Cost per lead, indexed to the account's own year</th><th>Account-months</th></tr>
    <tr><td>April to June</td><td>1.12</td><td>16</td></tr>
    <tr><td>July to September</td><td>0.92</td><td>15</td></tr>
    <tr><td>October to December</td><td>0.89</td><td>10</td></tr>
    <tr><td>January to March</td><td>0.87</td><td>11</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The same firm pays roughly a quarter more per lead in late spring than it does in the first
    quarter. Everyone in this profession launches in spring because the phone feels busy then, and
    they buy their leads at the top of the market. If your plan has any flexibility about when it
    starts, that is where the flexibility is worth spending. The longer read is in <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#spring-is-the-expensive-quarter">why spring is the expensive quarter</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The plan forks at residential and commercial</h2>
  <p>
    Ten of the 21 accounts carry a segment label, seven residential and three commercial, so treat
    the next paragraph as a direction rather than a benchmark. It is still the only segment split we
    have seen published anywhere for this profession.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Segment</th><th>Cost per lead</th><th>Lead to booked</th><th>Cost per booked consultation</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Residential (n=7)</td><td>$39.92</td><td>34.8%</td><td>$114.65</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Commercial (n=3)</td><td>$53.43</td><td>43.2%</td><td>$123.63</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    A commercial lead costs about a third more and books noticeably better, so the two land within
    about 8% of each other by the time you count a consultation on the calendar. Firms doing
    commercial work routinely assume they are priced out of paid acquisition. On this data they are
    paying roughly the same for a conversation.
  </p>
  <p>
    What changes is the argument, not the budget. If you sell to private clients, the plan leans on
    feeling, process and reassurance, which is <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a>. If you sell to developers, owners and business tenants, it leans on returns, timelines and de-risking, which is <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a>. Same channel, different creative and a longer follow-up window.
  </p>

  <h2>The line most plans get wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Where the lead is captured moved cost more than any targeting decision we looked at. Campaigns
    that collected the lead in an in-platform form averaged $40.55 per lead across 39 campaigns and
    20 accounts. Campaigns that sent people to a landing page first averaged $70.18 across 9
    campaigns and 7 accounts. About 78% of all leads in the dataset arrived through a form.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a 73% difference on the same spend, decided by one setting. Landing pages still earn
    their place when you need qualification or a download, and the tradeoff is set out in <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#lead-forms-against-landing-pages">lead forms against landing pages</a>. Put the choice in the plan deliberately instead of inheriting whatever the last person set up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second line most plans get wrong is sequencing. A firm with no demand coming in funds a
    website rebuild first, because it is the concrete thing. Your site makes people who already
    found you feel safe about inquiring, which is real work and covered in <a href="/website-design-for-architects">website design for architects</a>, and it finds nobody on its own. Fund the thing that brings people to it, then improve what they land on.
  </p>

  <h2>What this plan cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It stops at the booked consultation, on purpose. Across 1,023 booked consultations in our
    client-side data, 48 carry any recorded outcome and 18 of the 24 tracked calendars record none
    at all. A show rate calculated on that would be a statistic about our record keeping. Anyone
    quoting you a projection from ad spend to signed projects is filling that gap with an assumption
    and usually not saying so.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also says nothing about the channels that compound rather than convert. Search and the
    written pages behind it take months to move and then keep working, which is <a href="/seo-for-architects">SEO for architects</a>, and the relationships that generate referrals need their own system rather than good intentions, which is <a href="/referral-systems-for-architects">referral systems for architects</a>. Both belong in a twelve-month plan. Neither will fill next quarter.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>How do you write a marketing plan for an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    Start from the cost you can accept per booked consultation, work back to a monthly budget at a
    realistic cost per lead, pick one channel that can deliver at that price, and commit ninety days
    to it. Then write down the two review dates and what would make you change course. A plan that
    names a channel without naming a price is a list of intentions.
  </p>
  <h3>What percentage of revenue should an architecture firm spend on marketing?</h3>
  <p>
    The 5 to 10 percent figure circulating in AI answers is not stated in three of the four sources
    those answers cite, and the one that does carry a range cites nothing for it. A more useful
    version: spend what returns more than it costs, measured at the booked consultation, and cap it
    at what your studio can actually deliver on. A median month in our data was about $1,450.
  </p>
  <h3>How long before a marketing plan brings in work?</h3>
  <p>
    Leads come quickly on paid channels. Median time to first lead was one day and 17 of 21 accounts
    had one inside a week. Signed projects follow your own sales cycle, which for commercial work
    can run many months. Organic search and content sit on a different clock entirely and should be
    funded as the compounding layer rather than the engine.
  </p>
  <h3>What should a small architecture firm's marketing plan include?</h3>
  <p>
    One acquisition channel funded properly, a capture method chosen on purpose, a follow-up
    commitment with a time on it, and the four numbers above tracked monthly. Everything else is
    optional in year one. Firms lose more to running five channels at a tenth of the required budget
    than to picking the wrong one.
  </p>
  <h3>Does the plan change for a commercial architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    The economics barely move. Commercial leads cost more and book better, landing within about 8%
    of residential on cost per booked consultation. What changes is the creative argument, the
    number of people involved in the decision, and how long you keep showing up before the shortlist
    exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Put a price on every line</h2>
  <p>
    The plans on the first page of Google are not wrong about what to do. They are silent about what
    it costs, which leaves every firm owner to guess at the only variable that decides whether the
    plan is affordable. Take the four numbers above, set them against what a project is worth to
    you, and you have a year you can actually argue with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where all of this connects, from first ad to booked consultation, is laid out in <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects#what-a-system-actually-looks-like">lead generation for architects</a>, and the channel most of these numbers came from is <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects#what-it-actually-costs-with-real-numbers">Meta ads for architects</a>. If you would rather compare the firms who do this for a living before committing a year, we wrote that comparison too, in <a href="/best-marketing-agencies-for-architects">the best marketing agencies for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you would rather have the plan built and run for you while you stay on site visits, that is
